/*!
    \page vscodeext-getting-started.html
    \previouspage index.html
    \nextpage vscodeext-overview.html

    \title Getting started

    To check that \QVSC can find your Qt installation and CMake kits, open a Qt
    example for building and running.

    \image {vscodeext-getting-started.gif} {Building and running a Qt example}
    \caption Building and running a Qt example in VS Code.

    To open and run a Qt example project in VS Code:

    \list 1
        \li Install \QVSC.
        \li Open a folder that contains a Qt example (that has a
            \c {CMakeLists.txt} file) from your Qt installation.
        \li Go to \uicontrol {Command Palette}, and select
            \uicontrol {Qt: Register Qt installation} to register a Qt version.
        \li Go to \uicontrol {Command Palette} and select
            \uicontrol {Qt: Set the recommended Qt Extension settings} to show
            the registered Qt CMake kits on the status bar.
        \li Go to \uicontrol {Command Palette} and select
            \uicontrol {Qt: Scan for kits}. Then select a kit that matches your
            Qt version and toolchain.
        \li Select
            \inlineimage {run_project.png} {Launch the selected target in the terminal window}
            on the status bar.
        \li Select the executable as the launch target to build and run the
            project.
    \endlist

    You can now select a tutorial to follow to learn about developing
    Qt applications. Use project wizard templates to create your own
    application projects.

    \sa {Installation}, {Register Qt installations}, {Tutorials},
    {Create Qt projects}
*/
